{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Background There is a significant increase in the number of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection reports in various countries. However, the trend of reinfection rate over time is not clear.",
      "reason": "The abstract and introduction explicitly describe the current state (increasing reinfection reports) and the key uncertainty (the unclear trend of reinfection rate over time)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "The results of this study could help guide public health measures and vaccination strategies in response to the Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic.",
      "reason": "The manuscript states the practical importance and need for the review by linking findings to guiding public health measures and vaccination strategies."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "The advantage of this study was that compared with the previous meta-analysis of the SARSCoV-2 reinfection rate, first, to avoid the heterogeneous impact of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection definition on SARSCoV-2 reinfection rates, so this study defined reinfection and included only literature with reinfection intervals greater than 90 days.",
      "reason": "The discussion explicitly compares this review to previous meta-analyses and explains how the current review differs and why it was conducted (e.g., harmonized reinfection definition), justifying the need for the current review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review addresses time-varying reinfection rates (epidemiological incidence), not effects of interventions; therefore describing an intervention mechanism is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not evaluate complex interventions or delivery contexts and does not present or require a logic model; it focuses on reinfection rates over time."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Therefore, the aim of this study was to conduct a meta-analysis to describe and summarize the current reinfection rates in different regions and among diverse populations. Furthermore, the study sought to examine trends in reinfection rates over time, so as to provide reference for the development of global unified reinfection standards and reinfection measures in the future.",
      "reason": "The review objectives are explicitly stated (to describe/summarize reinfection rates and examine trends over time), but no relevant question-formulation framework (e.g., PICO or another formal framework) is referenced or used in the provided evidence, so the requirement is only partially met."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "We searched PubMed, Web of Science, Medline, Embase, Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials, China National Knowledge Infrastructure, and Wanfang for cohort studies, case-control studies, and cross-sectional studies up to March 16, 2023, to conduct a meta-analysis of global SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rate.",
      "reason": "This review concerns estimation of reinfection rates (observational studies) rather than effects of interventions; planned comparisons using PICO (for intervention effects) do not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The inclusion criteria were: (1) Conformed to research questions, i.e., studies that estimated the reinfection rate of SARS-CoV-2 or we could calculate it based on the data provided in the paper; (2) The study design included cohort study, case-control study, and descriptive study; (3) The study fited our working definition of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection... reinfection working definition in this study was based on a positive laboratory result at least 90 days after laboratory confirmation of primary infection (laboratory testing methods include reverse transcription-polymerase chain reaction or rapid antigen test...); (4) Original research. The exclusion criteria were: (1) The sample size was less than 20; (2) Belonged to one of the following types of studies: editorial, case report, case series study, systematic review, meta-analysis, animal experiment, news report; (3) Secondary reporting or articles on repeated studies of the same population; (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies study characteristics used to determine eligibility: target question (reinfection rate), eligible study designs (cohort, case-control, descriptive/cross-sectional), minimum sample size (<20 excluded), requirement for original research, and a working definition of reinfection (>=90 days with specified test types)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "To avoid language bias, the search was not limited to any language. We conducted preliminary screening based on the English title and English abstract provided by nonEnglish articles. Further, in the screening process, if the included article was neither in English nor in Chinese and did not provide an English abstract or full text, we used an online translator (Google Translate) for translation. There were no non-English articles in the final articles included in this study... The exclusion criteria were: ... (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The review reports language eligibility (no language limit; translation approach) and excludes grey literature (publication status). However, it does not specify restrictions by dissemination year, so the criterion is only partially addressed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "One of them reported only the reinfection rate, but not the associated number of first infections as well as the number of reinfections, and two other articles studied the same population, so the meta-analysis of single-group rates could not be performed. Therefore, we excluded the above literature with the number of unreported infections and selected one of two literature with the same population for inclusion.",
      "reason": "The review explicitly distinguishes and documents excluding studies because necessary outcome data (number of first infections/number of reinfections) were not reported, separate from other exclusion reasons."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_10",
      "quote": "Subgroup analyses were performed for age, reinfection variant, country, study population, and study type respectively, and the results were shown in Table 1... Subgroup analysis of the study types revealed that the SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rate in the crosssectional study was 2.13%... Thus, reinfection variant, country, and study type may be significant influencing factors for study heterogeneity.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies groups used in syntheses (age, reinfection variant, country, study population, study type) and links subgroup analyses to comparisons and heterogeneity exploration."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "reinfection working definition in this study was based on a positive laboratory result at least 90 days after laboratory confirmation of primary infection... advised from WHO and US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention [21, 22]. ... The exclusion criteria were: (1) The sample size was less than 20; ... (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The authors provide rationale for the chosen reinfection definition (based on WHO and CDC guidance). However, other notable restrictions (e.g., minimum sample size <20, exclusion of grey literature) are listed but not accompanied by explicit rationales within the provided evidence, so rationales are only partially provided."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "85"
}

